{
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "incident_type",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.85,
      "source_groundedness": 0.95,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Categorizing incidents (printer, hardware, software, connectivity) gives a genuine grouping axis to compare resolution efficiency across types. Values are soundly extracted from short_description (e.g. 'Printer not working' -> printer, 'Internet connectivity lost' -> connectivity)."
    },
    {
      "name": "description_clarity",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.75,
      "source_groundedness": 0.7,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Vague descriptions plausibly correlate with inefficient resolution, giving analytical leverage. Values are inferable from text terseness, though the boundary is somewhat subjective; 'Printer not working properly' being False is a defensible reading. Slight subjectivity lowers groundedness modestly."
    },
    {
      "name": "upstream_dependency",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.8,
      "source_groundedness": 0.75,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Whether an incident depends on upstream systems is a relevant efficiency signal (dependencies delay resolution). 'Unable to connect to the database' -> True is a warranted inference; most rows False is reasonable. Some inference risk but generally grounded in the description text."
    },
    {
      "name": "problem_recurrence",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.6,
      "source_groundedness": 0.55,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "Recurrence is potentially useful for efficiency analysis, but the value 'recurring_device_type' appears to be assigned merely because the incident is a printer/common device type, not from evidence of actual recurrence in the source text. This conflates category with recurrence and asserts recurrence the text does not directly support, weakening groundedness."
    }
  ],
  "analytical_usefulness": 0.78,
  "source_groundedness": 0.74,
  "rationale": "The added columns provide meaningful new axes (type, clarity, dependency, recurrence) for analyzing resolution inefficiency, going beyond the raw ITSM fields. Most values are soundly read from short_description. The main concern is problem_recurrence, which seems to infer recurrence from device commonality rather than actual textual evidence of repeat issues, introducing mild hallucination. Overall the set meets both necessary conditions reasonably well."
}

